Env preconfig green (58 tests, including the pre-existing ones). Now entry point 2 — startup.dart (AC3/H2 + L9/E2). Tests first, in test/cli/startup_test.dart — new group inside `resolveEffectiveCliArgs`:

1. H2: saved anthropic binding (@ api.anthropic.com, claude model) + explicit `--model gpt-5` without --provider → throws ConfigException naming both sides (gpt-5 + anthropic).
2. Explicit --model WITH explicit --provider → no joint validation against saved (manual control). E.g. --provider openai --model gpt-5 over saved anthropic → fine.
3. Explicit --model matching the saved family → fine (claude model on saved anthropic).
4. L9: endpoint-conflict degrade with a foreign saved baseUrl drops the stale model+baseUrl: saved copilot @ foreign URL + model x → resolved.args.model null + args.baseUrl null (and provider degrades). And the codex case (baseUrl == degraded kind's default) keeps model/baseUrl.
5. Preconfig model: FA preconfig provides model → binding validated (preconfig path already validated at parse).

Note: `resolveEffectiveCliArgs` currently never throws. Adding a throw for the flag-mismatch: headless boot catches ConfigException → `_fail`. Let me check the signature/doc: "Throws [ConfigException] on a malformed declaration" is on faProviderPreconfig. I'll extend the doc.

Implementation in resolveEffectiveCliArgs:

```dart
final modelId = parsed.model ?? faPreconfig?.modelId ?? saved.modelId;
final baseUrl = parsed.baseUrl ?? faPreconfig?.baseUrl ?? saved.baseUrl;
```

becomes:

```dart
final degraded = restore.endpointConflict && !explicitOverride;
// L9/E2: the degraded kind must not inherit the stale pair — keep the
// saved baseUrl+model only when they still cohere with the DEGRADED
// kind's own endpoint; otherwise the poison is dropped.
final savedBaseUrlKept = !degraded || sameEndpoint(saved.baseUrl, <degraded-kind default>);
```

Wait, what's the degraded kind? `provider` when restoredKind == null → `_bootProvider` falls to parsed.provider (the default, 'openai-completions'). The degraded spec = resolveCliProviderSpec(parsed.provider)… hmm, provider is `_bootProvider(...)` which with restoredKind null returns faPreconfig?.spec.kind ?? parsed.provider. The degraded kind's default endpoint: resolveCliProviderSpec(provider)?.defaultBaseUrl. Keep saved pair iff sameEndpoint(saved.baseUrl, degradedDefault). CliConfig.baseUrl is non-null (default openrouter). 

```dart
final degradedDefault = resolveCliProviderSpec(provider)?.defaultBaseUrl;
final keepSavedPair = !degraded ||
    (degradedDefault != null && sameEndpoint(saved.baseUrl, degradedDefault));
final modelId = parsed.model ?? faPreconfig?.modelId ?? (keepSavedPair ? saved.modelId : null);
final baseUrl = parsed.baseUrl ?? faPreconfig?.baseUrl ?? (keepSavedPair ? saved.baseUrl : null);
```

Careful: `sameEndpoint(String, String)` — saved.baseUrl is String (non-null per CliConfig). And this "keep pair" logic also needs to not break the existing codex test (baseUrl openrouter == degraded default openrouter → kept ✓) and byName (127.0.0.1:9 → dropped ✓ — test only asserts provider/report fields, fine).

Then AC3:

```dart
if (parsed.model != null && !parsed.providerExplicit && faPreconfig == null) {
  ProviderModelBinding.resolve(
    providerKind: provider,
    modelId: parsed.model!,
    baseUrl: baseUrl,
    provenance: BindingProvenance.flag,
  ); // throws ConfigException naming both sides on a dialect mismatch
}
```

Hmm wait — one caveat: `provider` may be 'openai-completions' (the default) and baseUrl the saved openrouter — resolving 'openai-completions' with a custom baseUrl → openai spec; fine. With baseUrl dropped (degraded) and provider openai-completions → binding resolve throws "endpoint-shared needs baseUrl"! The M5 identity rule would make a DEGRADED boot with --model fail on the missing baseUrl even when the model is fine. But wait — when degraded and pair kept (codex case), baseUrl = saved openrouter → binding fine. When degraded and pair dropped: baseUrl null → binding throws 'endpoint-shared...'. Hmm — but that case would also make `_buildModel`→buildCliDefaultModel(provider) with model null throw later anyway. With an explicit --model over a dropped pair: binding throws the baseUrl error — confusing message. Better: run the binding check ONLY when baseUrl is non-null, OR pass the degraded default? The binding's identity check for shared kinds without baseUrl demands a record or baseUrl — for the boot path, the EXECUTABLE will fail at buildCliDefaultModel anyway... no wait, with --model given the model exists; the boot would proceed buildCliDefaultModel('openai-completions', model, baseUrl: null) → buildCatalogModel baseUrl null → openrouter default (buildCliDefaultModel → spec defaultBaseUrl). Hmm! So buildCliDefaultModel has its own default fallback — the boot rides openrouter. So for consistency: in the AC3 check, if the binding can't resolve for lack of identity (shared kind, no baseUrl), SKIP the validation? No — fail-loud is better: the endpoint-shared boot without baseUrl is exactly the M5 poison. But it would REJECT `fa --model x` boots on a fresh default install... wait, fresh install: CliConfig defaults baseUrl openrouter → kept (not degraded) → binding resolves openrouter default. OK. The dropped-pair degraded + explicit --model case: throw the binding's endpoint-shared error — acceptable (the config is broken; re-pin explicitly). Actually let me reconsider: dropping the pair happens when the saved baseUrl is foreign to the degraded kind. If the user ALSO passes --model but no --base-url, booting on the degraded kind's DEFAULT endpoint with the flagged model is reasonable (openai-completions @ openrouter). The binding error message would say "needs the record name or an explicit baseUrl" — actionable-ish. Hmm, but nicer: when the pair was dropped, use the degraded kind's default endpoint as the binding baseUrl (the boot will do exactly that via buildCliDefaultModel's spec default). Let me pass `baseUrl: baseUrl ?? (keepSavedPair ? null : degradedDefault)`. Wait if keepSavedPair false and baseUrl null → pass degradedDefault (non-null for openai-completions = openrouter). That resolves cleanly.

Actually simpler: compute `bindingBaseUrl = baseUrl ?? degradedDefault` inside the check.

Edge: `parsed.model` explicit + parsed.baseUrl explicit + saved google + model gpt-5 → provider google (saved), baseUrl flag → binding resolve(google @ flag-url...) → dialect checks. Good.

Also: does the binding check fire when the saved restore was UNKNOWN (unknownSavedProvider)? E.g. saved 'from-the-future' + --model gpt-5: provider = parsed default openai-completions, baseUrl = saved (default openrouter unless user's config had custom). resolveCliProviderSpec('openai-completions', baseUrl openrouter) → openrouter spec → binding: openai-dialect @ openrouter + gpt-5 → router endpoint, hint openai == spec api → pass. Good.

