Two failures:
1. My updated mapping test: `_entry('openai-completions', 'm-1', baseUrl: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1')` → model.provider expected 'openrouter', got 'openai'. Because `buildCatalogModel(spec.name, ...)` — spec from `catalogProvider(queueKindCatalogName('openai-completions', baseUrl))` → legacy rule with baseUrl == openrouter default → `resolveCliProviderSpec('openai-completions', baseUrl: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1')` → `_legacyOpenAiSpec(baseUrl != null)` → baseUrl non-null → openai spec! The legacy rule maps ANY non-null baseUrl (even the default) to 'openai'. Hmm wait — the ORIGINAL test passed '_entry('openai-completions', 'm-1')' (baseUrl null) → openrouter. With baseUrl set to the openrouter default, the legacy rule yields openai. The binding resolve: `resolveCliProviderSpec('openai-completions', baseUrl: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1')` → same openai spec → binding baseUrl = the explicit one (openrouter URL) — spec.api openai-completions vs endpoint... endpoint openrouter (openai-completions api) → pass. So the chain builds with spec openai — but buildCatalogModel('openai', 'm-1', baseUrl: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1') → model.provider 'openai' @ openrouter URL. To keep the ORIGINAL observable (provider 'openrouter' on the default endpoint), pass baseUrl to the legacy resolver as the BINDING's baseUrl but with the default-endpoint normalization: when entry.baseUrl sameEndpoint-matches the resolved spec's default, treat as null for the legacy lookup. Cleaner: in buildProviderQueueChain, normalize `entry.baseUrl` for the catalog lookup: `final lookupBaseUrl = (entry.baseUrl != null && spec != null && sameEndpoint(entry.baseUrl, spec.defaultBaseUrl)) ? null : entry.baseUrl`. 

Actually — the same normalization belongs INSIDE the binding: a binding whose explicit baseUrl equals the resolved spec's default endpoint should resolve the record WITHOUT the baseUrl (the default spelling must not flip openai-completions → openai; E1's spirit). Let me add that to ProviderModelBinding.resolve: after computing spec with the legacy rule... the legacy rule runs INSIDE resolveCliProviderSpec(baseUrl). To normalize: first try resolveCliProviderSpec(id) without baseUrl; if the resolved spec has a default endpoint and the explicit baseUrl sameEndpoint-matches it, use the no-baseUrl resolution. Implement in resolve:

```dart
var spec = resolveCliProviderSpec(id);
if (spec == null || (baseUrl != null && spec.defaultBaseUrl.isNotEmpty && sameEndpoint(baseUrl, spec.defaultBaseUrl))) {
  // retry without baseUrl so the default spelling keeps the record identity
  spec = resolveCliProviderSpec(id);
}
```
Simpler: 
```dart
final trimmedUrl = baseUrl?.trim() ?? '';
final isDefaultSpelling = trimmedUrl.isNotEmpty &&
    (() { final s = resolveCliProviderSpec(id); return s != null && s.defaultBaseUrl.isNotEmpty && sameEndpoint(trimmedUrl, s.defaultBaseUrl); })();
final spec = resolveCliProviderSpec(id, baseUrl: isDefaultSpelling ? null : baseUrl);
```

Then in buildProviderQueueChain, `buildCatalogModel(spec.name, entry.model, baseUrl: entry.baseUrl)` — spec is now openrouter for the default spelling → provider 'openrouter', baseUrl passed explicit openrouter → model.baseUrl openrouter. Test expects provider 'openrouter' ✓.

Also `queueKindCatalogName(binding.providerKind, entry.baseUrl)` → legacy rule with the openrouter URL → 'openai'... then catalogProvider('openai')! → openai spec → the chain builds with the WRONG spec again. Fix: normalize there too — pass null when the baseUrl is the default spelling. Let me restructure: compute in the loop:

```dart
final spec = catalogProvider(
  queueKindCatalogName(
    binding.providerKind,
    _defaultSpelling(entry.baseUrl, binding.providerKind) ? null : entry.baseUrl,
  ),
)!;
```

Hmm, getting clunky. The binding itself knows: binding.recordName... For openai-completions default spelling, binding.recordName is null (recordName only set when caller passes). Give the binding a way to expose the resolved RECORD: add `final String record` field? The binding already resolves to a spec internally. Add a `recordName`... it IS a field (caller-provided). Let me add `ProviderSpec get spec => resolveCliProviderSpec(recordName ?? providerKind, baseUrl: ...)`? Circularity again.

Cleanest: expose the resolved spec's NAME on the binding: `final String catalogRecord;` set from `spec.name` at resolve time. Then the queue uses `catalogProvider(binding.catalogRecord)!` — no legacy-rule re-derivation, byte-identical. And `buildCatalogModel(binding.catalogRecord, entry.model, baseUrl: entry.baseUrl)`.

Wait — but for the wire OVERRIDE case, spec.name is the RECORD's name while binding.providerKind is the overridden wire; factory(spec.kind,...) would use the record's kind, not the override. For the queue, wire overrides on entries... the queue kinds list has no override input anyway (no `wire` key in queue config). binding.providerKind == spec.kind always in the queue path. Fine — use spec.kind for the factory via catalogProvider(binding.catalogRecord).kind.

2. Second failure: the keyless entry SKIPS before the binding runs, so the identity error never fires when the key is missing. Order: the binding should validate BEFORE the skip? The skip exists so a queue with one dead entry still boots on the rest. But an INVALID entry should fail the whole queue (config error), not skip. Move the binding resolution BEFORE the key-stack check. Then an identity-less entry throws even without a key (correct: the config is invalid). Update: move binding above the stack check.

